Carry the model's own input-embedding postprocessing across the substitution

The Transformers backend replaces the model's input embedding wholesale with a
VocabParallelEmbedding. It recognizes exactly one form of custom behaviour --
a scalar `embed_scale` attribute, handled by ScaledVocabParallelEmbedding --
and silently drops anything else the original layer did.

MuseGlimmer's embedding is `MuseGlimmerTextNormedEmbedding`, whose forward is
`self.embed_norm(super().forward(input_ids))`, an unweighted RMSNorm over the
looked-up rows. It has no `embed_scale`, so the norm is discarded; and because
it is unweighted there is no weight left unloaded, so nothing warns. The whole
residual stream then starts at the wrong scale.

The failure is not subtle once seen but points nowhere near its cause. On
`turboderp/Muse-Glimmer-30B-exl3` @2.00bpw the model loads clean, runs at full
speed, and emits confident garbage: `<|eom|>` at logprob -0.18 where the intact
model gives ` to` (opening its `to=self` reasoning channel) at -0.0000 with the
runner-up 15.8 nats behind.

Deleting the same norm from native exllamav3 -- which models it explicitly --
reproduces vLLM's output token for token, all seven tokens of it including the
garbage, which is what identifies this as the whole cause rather than a
contributor.

Detection is structural, and deliberately narrow: a plain nn.Embedding owns no
submodules and does not override forward, so a subclass doing both is applying
that submodule to the lookup result. Only the single-submodule shape is
inferable; anything more elaborate is left alone rather than guessed at, which
preserves today's behaviour for every model that already worked. It logs when
it fires, since silently changing a model's arithmetic is the failure mode this
is fixing.

Nothing here is EXL3-specific -- an unquantized MuseGlimmer served with
`--model-impl transformers` has the same bug -- so it is a plausible upstream
contribution.

diff --git a/vllm/model_executor/models/transformers/base.py b/vllm/model_executor/models/transformers/base.py
index 35e5c9d1a..15c80b773 100644
--- a/vllm/model_executor/models/transformers/base.py
+++ b/vllm/model_executor/models/transformers/base.py
@@ -89,6 +89,39 @@ class ScaledVocabParallelEmbedding(VocabParallelEmbedding):
         return super().forward(input_) * self.embed_scale
 
 
+class PostprocessedVocabParallelEmbedding(VocabParallelEmbedding):
+    """`VocabParallelEmbedding` that re-applies a module the model's own input
+    embedding layer ran on top of the plain lookup."""
+
+    def __init__(self, *args, postprocess: nn.Module, **kwargs):
+        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)
+        self.postprocess = postprocess
+
+    def forward(self, input_: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:
+        return self.postprocess(super().forward(input_))
+
+
+def _embedding_postprocess(module: nn.Module) -> nn.Module | None:
+    """The module an `nn.Embedding` subclass applies to the rows it looks up.
+
+    A plain `nn.Embedding` owns no submodules and does not override `forward`.
+    A subclass that does both is applying that submodule to the lookup result:
+    MuseGlimmer's `MuseGlimmerTextNormedEmbedding`, whose `forward` is
+    `self.embed_norm(super().forward(input_ids))`, is the case this exists for.
+    That norm is unweighted, so substituting the layer otherwise drops it
+    without any weight going missing to warn about.
+
+    Only the single-submodule shape is inferable. Anything more elaborate is
+    left alone rather than guessed at.
+    """
+    if type(module).forward is nn.Embedding.forward:
+        return None
+    children = list(module.children())
+    if len(children) != 1:
+        return None
+    return children[0]
+
+
 class Base(
     nn.Module,
     VllmModel,
@@ -180,11 +213,23 @@ class Base(
                 quant_config=self.quant_config,
             )
             embed_scale = getattr(input_embeddings, "embed_scale", None)
+            postprocess = _embedding_postprocess(input_embeddings)
             if embed_scale is not None:
                 # Some models scale embeddings inside the input embedding layer
                 new_input_embeddings = ScaledVocabParallelEmbedding(
                     **embedding_kwargs, embed_scale=float(embed_scale)
                 )
+            elif postprocess is not None:
+                # Others normalize inside it (MuseGlimmer's `embed_norm`). That
+                # lives in the layer's `forward`, so substituting the layer
+                # drops it unless it is carried over explicitly.
+                logger.info(
+                    "Carrying over input embedding postprocess: %s",
+                    type(postprocess).__name__,
+                )
+                new_input_embeddings = PostprocessedVocabParallelEmbedding(
+                    **embedding_kwargs, postprocess=postprocess
+                )
             else:
                 new_input_embeddings = VocabParallelEmbedding(**embedding_kwargs)
             self.model.set_input_embeddings(new_input_embeddings)
